item.text:
I searched ASF’s 2022 consolidated matrix, individual and framework audits, CONEVAL program-monitoring records, the formal USTR consultation request, Mexico’s official response, Banxico records, and official replacements for the three imprecisely extracted sources. The strongest additions concern: actual central-bank independence under pressure; exact energy measures and treaty provisions alleged; Mexico’s December corrective work plan; contract/project-level Tren Maya observations; health-program design and procurement implementation; and Pemex’s documented response to debt warnings.

Important unresolved gaps remain. ASF’s public reports distinguish observations, amounts recovered during audit, and amounts pending clarification, but its searchable recovery annexes did not expose reliable audit-action-level final status for the cited Tren Maya and INSABI matters. Consequently, none of the outstanding amounts below is characterized as a final loss or proven misconduct. I also found no strong evidence that Ramírez de la O personally overruled AMLO in a contested 2022 decision; Banxico provides the clearest actual intervention-limit case, while Economy Ministry turnover and coordination problems provide adverse context but not proof about the finance minister’s autonomy. The December energy work plan establishes a response and attempted correction, not final resolution or a finding that Mexico breached USMCA.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Analysis: Mexican central bank answers López Obrador pressure with defiant autonomy","publisher":"Reuters, republished by Euronews","publication_date":"2022-07-06","url":"https://es.euronews.com/next/2022/07/06/mexico-bancocentral","claim":"During 2022 Banxico continued tightening monetary policy notwithstanding presidential pressure for lower rates. Concern over intervention intensified after López Obrador disclosed the March 24 rate increase before Banxico announced it. The episode supplies contemporaneous evidence that the governor and board retained operational decision-making independence, although the premature disclosure shows an intervention risk and does not establish autonomy for cabinet ministers.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and sections describing the March 24 premature disclosure, subsequent rate decisions, and board members’ defense of autonomy","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-026","canonical_fact_key":"amlo_2022_banxico_operational_autonomy_under_pressure","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.2"],"source_type":"independent_news_analysis","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters analysis identifies the contested episode and observable monetary-policy response; Banxico’s published decisions independently establish the rate actions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns decisions and presidential conduct in calendar 2022.","ruler_attribution":"AMLO personally disclosed the pending rate decision and had publicly pressed the bank; the board’s subsequent conduct belongs to the autonomous institution, not to AMLO.","contrary_evidence":"AMLO’s nominee Victoria Rodríguez Ceja led the bank, and the premature disclosure raised legitimate concerns; this is evidence of effective operational limits, not absence of presidential pressure.","underlying_fact_key":"amlo_2022_banxico_operational_autonomy_under_pressure","lenses":["5B.2"]}  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Autonomía","publisher":"Banco de México","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.banxico.org.mx/contactobanxico/autonomia.html","claim":"Banxico’s institutional framework gives its board management, budgetary, and operational autonomy; staggered fixed terms, removal only for serious cause, and constitutional authority to choose monetary-policy instruments limit presidential intervention. This explains the legal mechanism through which AMLO’s appointees could act independently in 2022, but does not by itself prove how they acted.","locator":"Sections headed “Autonomía” and “Autonomía de gestión,” including the quotation from Constitution article 28 and the paragraphs on staggered appointments, tenure, and removal","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-027","canonical_fact_key":"amlo_2022_banxico_legal_intervention_limits","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.2"],"source_type":"primary_institutional_legal_explanation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official central-bank explanation tied directly to Constitution article 28 and the Banco de México Law.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"The cited institutional rules governed throughout 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The constraints legally limited AMLO’s authority over the board after appointment; they are inherited constitutional safeguards rather than reforms attributable to him.","contrary_evidence":"Formal protection cannot alone exclude informal political pressure, information leakage, or influence through appointments.","underlying_fact_key":"amlo_2022_banxico_legal_intervention_limits","lenses":["5B.2"]}  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Request for Consultations Pursuant to Articles 31.2 and 31.4 of the USMCA","publisher":"Office of the United States Trade Representative","publication_date":"2022-07-20","url":"https://ustr.gov/sites/default/files/US%20Cons%20Req%20Mexico%20energy_072022_0.pdf","claim":"The formal request precisely identifies four challenged groups of measures: 2021 Electricity Industry Law articles 26, 4(VI), 101 and 108(V); permit delays, denials, suspensions and revocations; Pemex’s exclusive extension under CRE resolution RES/1817/2019 to meet diesel sulfur rules; and SENER official letter SENER.100/195/2022 directing preferential gas-supply arrangements. USTR alleged apparent inconsistency with specified USMCA provisions, including articles 2.3, 2.11, 14.4, 22.5.2 and 29.3. These are formal allegations initiating consultations, not an adjudicated treaty breach.","locator":"PDF pp. 1–5: §I ¶¶1–2; §II ¶¶3–9; §III ¶¶10–11; §IV ¶¶12–13, especially pp. 1 lines 7–25 and p. 4 lines 123–165 in extracted text","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-028","canonical_fact_key":"mexico_2022_usmca_energy_consultation_measures","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.4"],"source_type":"primary_intergovernmental_legal_filing","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The underlying formal consultation request provides exact measures, instruments, dates, and treaty provisions while clearly using allegation language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Filed July 20, 2022 and includes the June 2022 gas directive and the legal status of other measures in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The electricity-law amendment originated as AMLO’s preferred initiative and the challenged energy orientation was an executive priority; particular permit actions and regulatory instruments belong to SENER, CRE, CENAGAS, CFE and Pemex unless separately traced to him.","contrary_evidence":"No panel ruling established breach, and Mexico disputed the characterization and pursued consultations.","underlying_fact_key":"mexico_2022_usmca_energy_consultation_measures","lenses":["5B.4"]}  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"La Secretaría de Economía explica Plan de Trabajo con Estados Unidos y Canadá para resolver consultas de T-MEC","publisher":"Secretaría de Economía, Government of Mexico","publication_date":"2022-12-12","url":"https://www.gob.mx/se/prensa/la-secretaria-de-economia-explica-plan-de-trabajo-con-estados-unidos-y-canada-para-resolver-consultas-de-t-mec?idiom=es","claim":"By December 2022 Mexico had established interagency working groups with the United States and Canada, was reviewing the status of permits to advance responses, and coordinated Economy and Energy officials in pursuit of an amicable settlement. This is dated evidence of an administrative response and willingness to examine implementation issues, but it records neither withdrawal of the challenged rules nor final settlement.","locator":"Opening description and final paragraphs, particularly the work-plan item “Revisión de permisos para conocer el estatus y avanzar en su respuesta” and the statement on close SE–SENER coordination","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-029","canonical_fact_key":"mexico_2022_energy_consultation_corrective_response","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.4","5B.7"],"source_type":"primary_government_response","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official, dated Mexican account of concrete response steps; its self-reporting and lack of final outcomes require restrained use.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published and describes actions underway in December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The response was undertaken by AMLO’s Economy and Energy ministries; the source does not say AMLO personally ordered particular corrections.","contrary_evidence":"The consultations remained unresolved, and reviewing permits is not proof that delayed or denied permits were granted or that challenged rules were corrected.","underlying_fact_key":"mexico_2022_energy_consultation_corrective_response","lenses":["5B.4","5B.7"]}  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Matriz de Datos Básicos del Informe del Resultado de la Fiscalización Superior de la Cuenta Pública 2022, Tercera Entrega","publisher":"Auditoría Superior de la Federación","publication_date":"2024-02-20","url":"https://www.asf.gob.mx/Trans/Informes/IR2022c/Documentos/Matriz/IR2022_Entrega_a.pdf","claim":"ASF’s project-level matrix reports mixed Tren Maya findings. Audit 115 for Tramo 5 Norte recorded one result cured during audit, no outstanding action, and no quantified amount. Audit 116 for Tramo 5 Sur recorded four results with actions and MXN2.1538 million pending clarification. Audits 111–113 for Tramos 1–3 recorded respectively MXN57.5688 million, MXN28.8349 million, and MXN65.4666 million pending clarification, with no recoveries recorded at report issuance. Audit 118 on project financing and expenditures recorded MXN10.0193 million pending clarification. These are audit observations or unresolved clarification amounts, not final proven losses.","locator":"Tourism-sector rows for audits 111, 112, 113, 115, 116 and 118 in the third-delivery basic-data matrix; columns “Resultados,” “Acciones,” “Recuperaciones Totales,” and “Montos por Aclarar”","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-030","canonical_fact_key":"asf_2022_tren_maya_project_audit_status_at_issuance","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.5","5B.6","5B.7"],"source_type":"primary_supreme_audit_matrix","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ASF matrix supplies audit-specific status and monetary categories, but not later action-level final resolution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Audits fiscal-year 2022 expenditure; report issued later as required by the audit cycle.","ruler_attribution":"The Tren Maya was AMLO’s flagship executive project, supporting attribution for project selection and tolerance of its implementation structure; individual payments and control failures belong to implementing entities unless evidence traces them personally to him.","contrary_evidence":"The matrix records favorable outcomes as well: Tramo 5 Norte had no outstanding action or quantified amount, and some findings elsewhere were cured during audit. Pending clarification is not a finding of loss or corruption.","underlying_fact_key":"asf_2022_tren_maya_project_audit_status_at_issuance","lenses":["5B.5","5B.6","5B.7"]}  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Marco de Referencia: Programa de Atención a la Salud, Cuenta Pública 2022","publisher":"Auditoría Superior de la Federación","publication_date":"2024-02-20","url":"https://www.asf.gob.mx/Trans/Informes/IR2022c/Documentos/Auditorias/MR-E023_a.pdf","claim":"Across 2022 E023 health-program audits, 265 of 309 observations were resolved during audit and 44 retained actions. States initially had MXN586.2 million observed, then clarified MXN331.3 million and reimbursed MXN18.8 million during audit, leaving MXN236.1 million pending clarification. For INSABI, ASF reported MXN321.0 million pending clarification, principally involving unauthorized payments to temporary workers and payments to contractors for maintenance services ASF said were not performed. The remaining sums were unresolved audit amounts, not final adjudicated losses or proof of corruption.","locator":"Section “Montos por aclarar y recuperaciones operadas,” including the observation-status graphic and paragraphs immediately following the MXN557.1 million aggregate; approximately report pp. 25–27","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-031","canonical_fact_key":"asf_2022_e023_health_contract_audit_status","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.5","5B.6","5B.7"],"source_type":"primary_supreme_audit_framework_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ASF synthesis separates resolved observations, reimbursements, and pending clarification while identifying the principal INSABI implementation problems.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly audits fiscal-year 2022 health-program implementation; later publication specifically establishes 2022 facts.","ruler_attribution":"INSABI and the free-health program were central administration choices under AMLO, supporting administration-level implementation attribution; the source does not show that AMLO selected contractors or authorized questioned payments.","contrary_evidence":"Most observations were resolved during audit, states clarified or reimbursed substantial amounts, and the report does not establish final loss or personal misconduct.","underlying_fact_key":"asf_2022_e023_health_contract_audit_status","lenses":["5B.5","5B.6","5B.7"]}  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Informe de monitoreo de programas prioritarios 2022","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/InformesPublicaciones/Documents/Informe_Programas_prioritarios_2022.pdf","claim":"CONEVAL found a specific implementation-design regression in the administration’s priority health program U013: its central purpose had shifted from effective, full and equitable health and medicine access to transferring resources to states; it lacked a clear target population and was one of only two priority programs whose deliverables did not clearly connect to durable improvements in beneficiaries’ lives. At the same time, U013’s real budget had increased by MXN61.5232 billion, or 1,336.3%, from 2019 to 2022. This links health-access implementation quality to choices in program design and monitoring rather than inherited conditions alone.","locator":"PDF pp. 16–17, lines 292–330 for budget growth; p. 25, lines 588–602 for the change in purpose; pp. 27–28, lines 628–660 for target-population weakness; p. 32, lines 775–790 for unclear deliverables and results linkage","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-032","canonical_fact_key":"coneval_2022_u013_health_program_design_implementation","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.6","5B.7"],"source_type":"independent_statutory_program_evaluation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CONEVAL is the statutory independent evaluator and provides precise program, comparison-year, and page-level findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The evaluation specifically compares the administration’s program design through 2022.","ruler_attribution":"U013 was designated and funded as an administration priority, allowing administration-level ownership of its design trajectory; the record does not establish AMLO personally drafted its indicators.","contrary_evidence":"The administration greatly increased the program’s real budget, and CONEVAL found improvements among several other priority programs; weak design and measurement do not alone prove poor clinical outcomes.","underlying_fact_key":"coneval_2022_u013_health_program_design_implementation","lenses":["5B.6","5B.7"]}  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Deuda, Pemex Corporativo, Cuenta Pública 2022, Auditoría 209","publisher":"Auditoría Superior de la Federación","publication_date":"2023-10-31","url":"https://www.asf.gob.mx/Trans/Informes/IR2022b/Documentos/Auditorias/2022_0209_a.pdf","claim":"ASF found no irregularity in 12 of 14 reviewed results and issued two recommendations concerning Pemex debt management. Pemex responded with evidence that its business plan contained the objective of strengthening internal finances and meeting financing needs, a strategy of zero real net indebtedness during the administration, and alignment with its global financing proposal. The audit also recorded adverse liquidity and refinancing conditions following rating downgrades. This is dated evidence that the administration and Pemex formally responded to debt-management warnings, though it does not prove that the response corrected Pemex’s structural financial weakness.","locator":"PDF p. 26, final paragraphs of Result 14 and “Resumen de Resultados, Observaciones y Acciones”; quoted response dated 26 April 2023 concerning the 2022 business plan and debt strategy","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-033","canonical_fact_key":"asf_2022_pemex_debt_warning_and_response","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.6","5B.7"],"source_type":"primary_supreme_audit_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official audit gives both adverse financial context and the entity’s documented response, while clearly reporting the limited number and type of actions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Audits Pemex’s 2022 debt management; the 2023 response specifically documents the plan and strategy governing 2022.","ruler_attribution":"AMLO’s administration set Pemex’s state-centered strategy and supplied fiscal support, but operational debt controls and the formal audit response belong to Pemex management and Hacienda.","contrary_evidence":"Most reviewed results showed no irregularity, and Pemex documented planning and debt objectives; two recommendations do not establish loss or misconduct.","underlying_fact_key":"asf_2022_pemex_debt_warning_and_response","lenses":["5B.6","5B.7"]}  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Resolución del Consejo de Representantes que fija los salarios mínimos vigentes a partir del 1 de enero de 2022","publisher":"Diario Oficial de la Federación / Comisión Nacional de los Salarios Mínimos","publication_date":"2021-12-08","url":"https://www.dof.gob.mx/nota_detalle_popup.php?codigo=5637615","claim":"The legally operative resolution confirms that the tripartite CONASAMI Council fixed 2022 minimum wages after considering worker, employer and government positions. The general wage became MXN172.87 per day in most of Mexico and MXN260.34 in the northern border zone; professional minimum wages also rose 22%. This upgrades WEB-5B-005 with a directly accessible primary legal locator.","locator":"Resolution heading and operative provisions PRIMERO through TERCERO; tables of general and professional minimum wages effective January 1, 2022","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-034","canonical_fact_key":"mexico_2022_minimum_wage_tripartite_resolution","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.1","5B.2","5B.3"],"source_type":"primary_legal_resolution","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Gazette text is the binding underlying instrument and removes the prior access-limited locator problem.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The resolution took effect throughout calendar 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The increase aligned with AMLO’s wage policy, but the legal decision was made by the tripartite CONASAMI Council and should not be represented as a unilateral presidential act.","contrary_evidence":"The resolution establishes the wage rule, not its causal contribution to national income or poverty outcomes, especially among informal workers.","underlying_fact_key":"mexico_2022_minimum_wage_tripartite_resolution","lenses":["5B.1","5B.2","5B.3"]}  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Puestos de trabajo afiliados al Instituto Mexicano del Seguro Social","publisher":"Instituto Mexicano del Seguro Social","publication_date":"2023-01-09","url":"https://www.imss.gob.mx/sites/all/statics/i2f_news/PR%20009_0.pdf","claim":"IMSS’s original downloadable bulletin reports 21,372,896 affiliated jobs at the end of December 2022, of which 86.6% were permanent and 13.4% temporary. It also records a December decline of 345,705 jobs. This upgrades WEB-5B-009 with a stable primary PDF and preserves both the year-end level and seasonal monthly contraction.","locator":"PDF p. 1, heading and first three bullets under “Puestos de trabajo afiliados al Instituto Mexicano del Seguro Social”","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-035","canonical_fact_key":"imss_2022_year_end_affiliated_jobs","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.8"],"source_type":"primary_administrative_statistical_release","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Original IMSS PDF provides an exact, stable locator and administrative totals.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Reports the closing administrative count for calendar 2022, published nine days later.","ruler_attribution":"The employment total is a shared national outcome affected by reopening, labor demand, formalization policy and external conditions; it should not be assigned as AMLO’s personal causal achievement.","contrary_evidence":"The bulletin also reports a 345,705-job December decline, and IMSS affiliation does not measure all employment or job quality.","underlying_fact_key":"imss_2022_year_end_affiliated_jobs","lenses":["5B.3","5B.8"]}
